GPUI asks wgpu for Vulkan and OpenGL together, and wgpu loads every backend
it is asked for the moment adapters are listed — whichever one is then
picked. On Mesa the OpenGL driver brings LLVM with it: in the shell that was
17 MB of libLLVM and 5 MB of libgallium mapped, an OpenGL context allocated,
and seven driver threads, all for an adapter that was listed and dropped,
because Vulkan is what gets picked wherever Vulkan works.

So the context for a window with none to share — the first, or the one after
a lost device — is made with Vulkan alone, and with OpenGL beside it only if
no Vulkan adapter can draw on the window. A machine that needs OpenGL still
gets it; one that does not never loads it.

